\textbf{Key observations:}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{All 10 uber-polya solutions use genuinely novel approaches}, distinct from both
  OpenAI's and the authors' strategies. This was achieved by explicitly blacklisting known
  approaches and requiring the pipeline to explore alternative mathematical frameworks.
\item \textbf{Python verification with iteration} caught and corrected errors during solving:
  e.g., P6's key spectral bound was computationally disproven and the proof restructured around
  a sparse--dense dichotomy; P1's relative entropy divergence was verified via setting-sun
  asymptotics; P3's detailed balance was confirmed to machine precision ($\sim 10^{-16}$).
\item \textbf{Problem 7} is a standout: our representation-ring argument ($\chi(\sigma) = 1 = 0$
  via Maschke's theorem) is simpler than both the authors' cobordism proof and OpenAI's
  false-lemma approach. This illustrates how novel paths can sometimes find cleaner proofs.
\item \textbf{Problem 6}: our sparse--dense approach achieves $c = 1/8$, a better constant than
  the authors' $c = 1/42$. OpenAI could not solve this problem at all.
\item The author solutions remain the gold standard in rigor and elegance, but the
  uber-polya pipeline demonstrates that LLMs can find \emph{mathematically distinct} proof
  strategies when constrained away from common patterns.
\end{itemize}

